     1	//! The two graphic ops (road map 10's G2a): `add_graphic_clip`, which
     2	//! places a clip drawn by a module in the package, and `set_graphic`, which
     3	//! patches what it is drawn from.
     4	//!
     5	//! What a graphic IS — its fields, the name rule, the side bound, the
     6	//! schema and props checks and the colour normalisation — is
     7	//! `model_graphic.rs`'s, and this module only composes them into ops. The
     8	//! placement tail is `edit_described.rs`'s `add_described_clip`, the one every
     9	//! sourceless clip is placed through, so a graphic clip obeys the overlap
    10	//! rule and the default five seconds exactly as a text or shape clip does.
    11	//!
    12	//! **Every argument is checked before the placement tail runs**, name
    13	//! first, so an op the table's bound probe sends reaches the side check on
    14	//! any project, whatever its tracks.
    15	//!
    16	//! The core never reads a module (it has no filesystem), so the `schema`
    17	//! a `props` patch is checked against arrives WITH the op (G-4). Without
    18	//! one — a replayed op, a caller that has none — only the shape is
    19	//! checked.
    20	//!
    21	//! `set_graphic` merges `props` key by key into the stored map: `insert`
    22	//! keeps an existing key's slot and appends a new one, and `null` removes
    23	//! with `shift_remove`, which keeps the other keys' order (`Map::remove`
    24	//! would swap the last key into the hole). `Project ==` cannot see an
    25	//! order change, so the Identity arm is unaffected either way, and only
    26	//! the written text shows it. With a `schema`, a `null` for a prop an
    27	//! action on the clip names stores the schema's default instead (spec §11
    28	//! G-34 (d)): the action's ramp keeps a start.
    29	
    30	use serde_json::{Map, Value};
    31	
    32	use crate::actions_graphic::names_prop;
    33	use crate::edit_action_graphic::schema_default;
    34	use crate::edit_described::Described;
    35	use crate::export::describe;
